{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"The structural argument points to a simple classification: a stable set exists only when \\(K\\) is even, and then it is uniquely the three allocations that give two players \\(K/2\\) each. I’m checking the completeness proof, including why no other internally stable configuration can work."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"printf '%s\\\\n' \\\"\"'$(date -u +%FT%TZ) [sol] Structural lemma proof: choose distinct x=(a,b,c), y=(a,b+d,c-d), d!=0. A point z off x1=a sharing a coordinate with both must be z+=(a+d,b,c-d) or z-=(a-d,b+d,c). They share no coordinate, so at most one may occur. Either such z shares a coordinate with a point w on x1=a only when w=x or y. Thus any non-slice internal set is exactly a triangle. Up/down forms follow taking coordinatewise minima. Ready to write full answer unless opus prefers ownership.\" >> /work/board.md; ls /work; cat /work/board.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
