proof: choose distinct x=(a,b,c), y=(a,b+d,c-d), d!=0. A point z off x1=a sharing a coordinate with both must be z+=(a+d,b,c-d) or z-=(a-d,b+d,c). They share no coordinate, so at most one may occur. Either such z shares a coordinate with a point w on x1=a only when w=x or y. Thus any non-slice internal set is exactly a triangle. Up/down forms follow taking coordinatewise minima. Ready to write full answer unless opus prefers ownership.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > /work/sol_proof.md <<'EOF'\n# Answer\n\nA stable set exists if and only if **K is even**. For K = 2h, the **only** stable set is\n\n\\\\[\nV_h=\\\\{(0,h,h),(h,0,h),(h,h,0)\\\\}.\n\\\\]\n\nAll coordinates here are in integer units; divide by K to obtain money shares.\n\n## Proof\n\nTwo distinct allocations are compatible (neither dominates the other) if and only if they agree in a coordinate. Indeed, if they agree in a coordinate, their differences in the remaining coordinates have opposite signs, so neither has two strict gains. If they agree in no coordinate, their three nonzero differences sum to zero, so two have the same sign, giving domination in one direction.\n\n### 1. Internally stable configurations\n\nEvery nonempty internally stable set is either contained in a coordinate slice, or is a three-point set of one of the following two forms, where m is a nonnegative integer triple, d is a positive integer, and e_i is the i-th unit vector:\n\n\\\\[\n\\\\tag{U}\\\\{m+d e_1,m+d e_2,m+d e_3\\\\},\\\\qquad \\\\sum_i m_i+d=K;\n\\\\]\n\\\\[\n\\\\tag{D}\\\\{m+d(e_2+e_3),m+d(e_1+e_3),m+d(e_1+e_2)\\\\},\\\\qquad \\\\sum_i m_i+2d=K.\n\\\\]\n\nTo prove this, singletons are already contained in a slice. Otherwise select two points. By compatibility and a permutation of coordinates they can be written\n\n\\\\[\nx=(a,b,c),\\\\qquad y=(a,b+d,c-d),\\\\quad d>0,\n\\\\]\n\nwhere we exchange their names if necessary. Any point outside the slice with first coordinate a must agree with each of x and y in one of the other two coordinates. Consequently it must be one of\n\n\\\\[\nz_+=(a+d,b,c-d),\\\\qquad z_-=(a-d,b+d,c).\n\\\\]\n\nThese two candidates agree in no coordinate, so at most one can belong to the set. Moreover, a point in the slice with first coordinate a agrees with either candidate in a coordinate only if it is x or y. Thus if the set leaves the slice, it consists of exactly x,y and one candidate. With z_+ it has form (U), with m=(a,b,c-d); with z_- it has form (D), with m=(a-d,b,c-d). These minima are nonnegative because the corresponding three points are allocations. This proves the classification.\n\n### 2. A stable set cannot be contained in a slice\n\nSuppose V is contained in the slice x_1=a. No point of this slice can dominate another, so external stability forces V to contain the entire slice.\n\nIf a<K, consider y=(a+1,K-a-1,0). Any x in the slice has x_1<y_1. To dominate y it would therefore need x_2>y_2 and x_3>y_3. Integrality would imply\n\n\\\\[\nx_2+x_3\\\\ge (K-a-1)+1+1=K-a+1,\n\\\\]\n\ncontrary to x_2+x_3=K-a. This outside point is undominated. If a=K, the slice is the singleton (K,0,0), which does not dominate the outside allocation (K-1,1,0). Coordinate permutations cover every slice. The empty set is also not externally stable.\n\n### 3. Upward triangles (U) cannot be stable for K≥2\n\nEvery allocation y satisfying y_i≥m_i for all i is undominated by (U): each vertex can exceed y in at most its one raised coordinate.\n\nIf d≥2, the allocation y=m+(d-1,1,0) satisfies these inequalities and is not a vertex, contradicting external stability.\n\nIf d=1, then K≥2 implies some m_i≥1. Let j,k be the other two indices and take\n\n\\\\[\ny=m-e_i+e_j+e_k.\n\\\\]\n\nThis is a nonnegative allocation of total K, outside the triangle. In coordinates j,k, its values equal the largest values attained by any vertex. Thus any vertex can improve on y in at most coordinate i, and y is again undominated. This excludes (U).\n\n### 4. Downward triangles (D) force the stated answer\n\nSuppose (D) is stable. If m_i≥1 for some i, write j,k for the other indices and consider the vertex v=m+d(e_j+e_k). The allocation\n\n\\\\[\ny=v-e_i+e_j\n\\\\]\n\nis feasible and lies outside the triangle, since y_j=m_j+d+1 exceeds every vertex's j-th coordinate. Also y_k=m_k+d is the maximum k-th coordinate among the vertices. Consequently no vertex can improve on y in either coordinate j or coordinate k; domination is impossible. This contradicts external stability.\n\nTherefore m=(0,0,0), so K=2d and the set must be V_d. Together with the previous steps, this proves that odd K admits no stable set and that even K admits at most the stated one.\n\n### 5. Verification of existence\n\nLet K=2h. Every pair of vertices of V_h agrees in a coordinate, so V_h is internally stable.\n\nLet y be an allocation outside V_h. At least two of its coordinates are strictly less than h. For if two coordinates were at least h, their sum and the total 2h would force those coordinates to equal h and the third to equal zero, making y a vertex. The vertex of V_h with h in the two coordinates strictly below h improves both of them, and therefore dominates y. This proves external stability and completes the proof.\nEOF\nprintf '%s\\\\n' \\\"\"'$(date -u +%FT%TZ) [sol] Complete proposed proof is /work/sol_proof.md. Please review; propose copying verbatim to answer.md once independently confirmed. No submission yet.\" >> /work/board.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
